  # Review: full-codebase-audit — 2026-10-30

  ## Summary
  Reviewed the entire backend (`backend/src/app`, `backend/tests`) and frontend
  (`frontend/src`) against `.claude/rules/*.md` and the CLAUDE.md
  architecture/standards. The codebase is in good shape overall: layering is
  respected in nearly all routers/services/repositories, Pydantic schemas
  consistently use `extra="forbid"`, ownership/role checks are enforced in the
  service layer, categories are data-driven (no hardcoded category logic), the
  extraction strategy interface is used correctly, and the frontend consistently
  follows the TanStack Query / Zustand / RHF+Zod / shared-Axios-client
  conventions with accessible form markup. All static checks pass except one
  `mypy --strict` error and a few dependency-vulnerability findings. Found 3
  mandatory issues, 5 recommended improvements, and 3 nitpicks — no
  business-logic-in-routers, no missing role checks, and no cross-tenant
  ownership leaks were found.

  ## Mandatory
  - `backend/src/app/dependencies/auth.py:32` — `get_current_user` runs a raw
  `select(User).where(User.email == email)` query directly against the session
  instead of calling `UserRepository.get_by_email` (which already exists in
  `backend/src/app/repositories/user_repository.py:17` and does exactly this).
  This violates backend-python.md ("Put all DB access in repositories/... Never
  query the DB from a router or service directly") and the CLAUDE.md one-way
  layering rule, and duplicates logic that already exists. Fix: inject/construct
  a `UserRepository` here and call `get_by_email`.
  - `backend/src/app/services/categorization_service.py:157`
  (`_suggest_via_llm`) — instantiates
  `anthropic.AsyncAnthropic(**client_kwargs)` directly inside the service method
  on every LLM-fallback call, rather than receiving the client via
  `Depends`/constructor injection. This violates backend-python.md: "Inject
  dependencies (DB session, current user, services, clients) via Depends — never
  instantiate external clients (HTTP, storage, LLM/OCR) inside business
  functions." Contrast with `ClaudeExtractor`
  (`backend/src/app/extraction/claude_extractor.py:89-96`), which correctly
  builds its client once in `__init__` and is wired via
  `Depends(get_extractor)`. Besides the layering violation, this also creates a
  new HTTP client per receipt upload, which is wasteful. Fix: build/inject the
  Anthropic client once (e.g. via a constructor param or a `Depends`-provided
  factory) and reuse it.
  - `backend/src/app/core/security.py:39` — `mypy --strict` fails here:
  `create_access_token` is declared `-> str` but returns `jwt.encode(...)`,
  which mypy infers as `Any` (`error: Returning Any from function declared to
  return "str" [no-any-return]`). This violates backend-python.md ("code must
  pass `mypy --strict`") and the Definition of Done. Fix: wrap the return in
  `str(...)` (as already done for `hash_password` on line 19) or add an explicit
  cast.

  ## Recommended
  - `backend/src/app/repositories/category_repository.py:7` —
  `CategoryRepository` imports `CategoryOption` from
  `app.services.categorization_service`, i.e. a repository depending on the
  services layer. This inverts the declared one-way dependency flow (`routers ->
  services -> repositories`). Recommend moving `CategoryOption` (and the
  `CategoryProvider` Protocol it supports) into `schemas/` or a dedicated port
  module so repositories don't import from services.
  - `backend/src/app/services/receipt_service.py:190-204` (`update`) —
  `ReceiptUpdate.category_id` is applied via `setattr` with no check that the
  category exists (or is active). SQLite does not enforce FK constraints by
  default here (no `PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON` in `db/session.py`), so an
  arbitrary/nonexistent `category_id` would silently persist a dangling
  reference. Recommend validating the category (via
  `CategoryRepository.get_by_id`, mirroring the existing `_get_or_404` pattern
  in `category_service.py`) before accepting it.
  - `backend/pyproject.toml` (direct deps `pyjwt>=2.9`, `pillow>=10.4`) —
  `pip-audit` (run against the active interpreter) flags the installed versions:
  `pyjwt 2.13.0` has multiple known CVEs (fixed in 2.14.0/2.15.0) and `pillow
  12.3.0` has numerous CVEs (fixed in 13.0.0). Both are direct project
  dependencies and used for security-sensitive code paths (JWT signing, image
  handling). Recommend bumping the minimum pins. Note: `pip-audit` also flagged
  many other packages (`mcp`, `opentelemetry-api`, `ujson`, `setuptools`, `pip`,
  `pyasn1`, `urllib3`, `werkzeug`) that are not declared dependencies of this
  project (`pyproject.toml` has no reference to them) — these appear to be
  ambient/tooling packages in the shared interpreter, not part of this project's
  footprint, and are not actionable findings here.
  - `frontend/package.json` — `npm audit` flags `react-router-dom@^6.26.0` (open
  redirect / SSR hydration advisories) and `zod@^3.23.8` (DoS via oversized
  constrained collections), both with fixed major versions available. Both are
  approved libraries per CLAUDE.md, so this is a version-bump decision rather
  than a library-choice issue; recommend scheduling the upgrade (noting both
  fixes are breaking-change major bumps per `npm audit`).
  - `frontend/src/features/auth/hooks/useAuth.ts:17-21` — in `useLogin`'s
  `mutationFn`, `setToken(token)` is called (persisting to localStorage via the
  Zustand `persist` middleware) *before* `authService.getCurrentUser()` has
  succeeded. If the `getCurrentUser` call then fails, a bearer token is left
  persisted in storage without a confirmed user/session, leaving the store in a
  partially-authenticated state. Recommend returning `{ token, user }` from
  `mutationFn` only after both calls succeed, and calling `setSession` solely in
  `onSuccess` (drop the early `setToken` call).

  ## Nitpick
  - `backend/src/app/models/receipt.py:41`,
  `backend/src/app/schemas/receipt.py:34`,
  `backend/src/app/extraction/validation.py:44` — `needs_review: bool` doesn't
  use one of the prescribed boolean prefixes (`is_`, `has_`, `should_`, `can_`)
  from backend-python.md. Used consistently throughout, so low priority (e.g.
  rename to `should_review` if ever touched).
  - `frontend/src/features/receipts/components/ReceiptReviewForm.tsx:96` — line
  is 101 characters, one over the 100-char limit in frontend-react.md
  (`helperText` ternary for the merchant-name field).
  - `backend/src/app/core/config.py:33` — the default dev `jwt_secret_key`
  ("change-me-in-development-only") is 29 bytes, which triggers PyJWT's own
  `InsecureKeyLengthWarning` (observed during the test run) for HS256's 32-byte
  recommendation. Harmless since `.env.example` already documents replacing it
  with "a long random value" for real use, but a longer placeholder would avoid
  the warning noise in dev/test logs.

  ## Checks run
  - ruff (`ruff check src tests` from `backend/`): pass — "All checks passed!"
  - mypy --strict (`mypy --strict src` from `backend/`): fail — 1 error,
  `src/app/core/security.py:39: Returning Any from function declared to return
  "str" [no-any-return]`.
  - bandit (`bandit -r src` from `backend/`): pass — no issues identified (1
  `nosec` B105 marker on a literal `"token_type": "bearer"` string in
  `auth_service.py`, correctly suppressed as a false positive, not a real
  hardcoded password).
  - pytest (`pytest -q` from `backend/`): pass — 115 passed, 1 xfailed (the
  known, documented regex-parsing limitation in `text_fallback_extractor.py`,
  per the pre-existing-items note — not newly flagged).
  - eslint (`npm run lint` from `frontend/`): pass — no output/errors.
  - tsc --noEmit (`npx tsc --noEmit` from `frontend/`): pass — no output/errors.
  - npm audit (`npm audit --omit=dev` from `frontend/`): 3 vulnerabilities (2
  moderate in `react-router`/`react-router-dom`, 1 high in `zod`) — see
  Recommended.
  - pip-audit (`pip_audit` from `backend/`, run against the active interpreter,
  no isolated project venv available): flags direct deps `pyjwt` and `pillow`
  with known CVEs (see Recommended), plus a long list of unrelated ambient
  packages not declared in `pyproject.toml` (treated as noise, not findings).

  ## Verdict
  3 mandatory issues (a repository-bypass in `dependencies/auth.py`, an
  un-injected LLM client in `categorization_service.py`, and a `mypy --strict`
  failure in `core/security.py`) must be fixed before merge; otherwise the
  codebase is clean and well-aligned with the project's layering, security,
  testing, and frontend-state-management rules.

